#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063964 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/id #uuid "4f78dc34-7cc7-5024-9641-b4ea74e4d155" :question/math-academy-id "e-3743" :question/problem "Find the $x$-values of the stationary points of $f(x) =  x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2$ and classify each point." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x)  = 4x^3 - 16 x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-16x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ x(x+2)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of $f''(x)$ at the critical points. Computing $f''(x),$ we get\n\n$$\nf''(x)= 12 x^2 - 16,\n$$\n\nand evaluating $f''(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)^{2}-16=32>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-16=-16<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-16=32>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0,$ so this is a relative maximum. On the other hand, the second derivative is positive at $x=\\pm 2,$ so these are relative minima."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063965 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/id #uuid "088bc8a4-e82a-586b-89b5-49482f579c4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3744" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = x^3-6x^2-15x.$ Given that $f'(5) = 0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f''(5)  >           0$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=5$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=  x^3-6x^2-15x,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) =  3x^2-12x-15\n$$\n\nWe're given that $f'(5) = 0.$ Indeed, we can see that $f'(5) = 3(5)^2 - 12(5) - 15 = 0.$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =6x-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(5) & =6(5)-12 \\\\  & =30-12 \\\\  & =18>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a38dba5-622e-5356-bae0-ac479825ebd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293836} {:answer/id #uuid "be64c842-1c2a-5e23-a999-aa07cfad96f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293837} {:answer/id #uuid "5c808dd9-e862-5d77-b630-69b56e7db2f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293838} {:answer/id #uuid "381f926c-73d2-55a8-b374-e171f87db6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293839} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf16a51-e154-5a7d-95d8-b1d2a62fdb49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a38dba5-622e-5356-bae0-ac479825ebd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293836} :answer-field/id #uuid "f29de4f2-d3d7-5c89-9a1e-6f0bd70b60fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "625bbe54-0b3f-5afb-b15e-eb3a5b309cd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-114415" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1+2x){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-1,0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=({x}^{2}+x)\\cdot {e}^{x}+(2x+1){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+3x+1){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so there is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is false. $x=1$ is not a critical point.\n\n- Statement IV is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b0353e-bafe-5d57-8d37-72e1c92e74db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303567} {:answer/id #uuid "591ddb1c-6d16-5956-8f0e-ff28e8c470f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303568} {:answer/id #uuid "e4731eb3-c838-5d5f-a4b2-516b9d92f14e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303569} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb347c2-9856-5f04-8482-c7ada82795cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303570} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c3dfe8-712a-5dde-a801-493afa1e462a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffb347c2-9856-5f04-8482-c7ada82795cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303570} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f403874-b77e-5453-b0dd-fc42a08f3eb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7424056-31be-5c03-b30d-76d3e1194cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-21500" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-3{x}^{5/2}+5{x}^{3/2}+15$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=1$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=1$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{5/2}+5{x}^{3/2}+15,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{3/2}+5\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}=-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2}(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}=-\\frac{45}{4}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{4}{x}^{-1/2},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(1)=-\\frac{45}{4}(1)^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{4}(1)^{-1/2}=-\\frac{15}{2}<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=1$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19740fc7-c059-56b6-9990-723f7ddf5bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061603} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea47769-4dd7-5ccd-a76a-54f114fca891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061604} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe77206-a1c3-5167-ac5f-f8ec0d636c6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061605} {:answer/id #uuid "7940920f-a322-5c3e-83f9-9af4d83c10bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061606} {:answer/id #uuid "7e85a37e-a2e2-5378-8b9d-19b70fd91053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19740fc7-c059-56b6-9990-723f7ddf5bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061603} :answer-field/id #uuid "2914e507-bc98-5466-90ad-8f67bf2f439d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fa08ecc-691c-5923-94fb-19ce5c958e14" :question/math-academy-id "q-21502" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-6x+12,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-1$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-1$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=-1$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-6x+12,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-6=6{x}^{2}-6,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ 6({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 6(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm 1.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=-1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-1)=-12<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=-1,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=-1$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=-1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c17c5262-a191-5f8f-809b-35a8463a2485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303574} {:answer/id #uuid "f8085f68-bcb5-5048-a333-8119c7f54e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303575} {:answer/id #uuid "772ca79f-acab-57f8-aac1-9b7ce244ec4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303576} {:answer/id #uuid "121e1b32-aa44-58a6-aeaa-2df101a75838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303577} {:answer/id #uuid "83525aaf-af2f-5586-b3d4-c0529c291449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83525aaf-af2f-5586-b3d4-c0529c291449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303578} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0216f1f-c5ba-5956-b2b3-3db49c2e1d2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d08953a7-b9c1-53e4-92d6-e017849bbea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-21508" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-6x+9.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-6x+9,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6x-6.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =6>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b24d93a5-c574-502e-8b49-eee4a7af58ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061612} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b4770d-54e4-55f5-8740-7594e0bbff53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061613} {:answer/id #uuid "09598d53-a609-5cbb-9cdc-1e6de3deaf3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061614} {:answer/id #uuid "0ebe97ff-1a3f-5ecc-80c2-7a6b3e2fcb6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061615} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9b87bb-d8c3-5c16-aa4b-838856c63fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09598d53-a609-5cbb-9cdc-1e6de3deaf3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061614} :answer-field/id #uuid "0907ab69-0c09-5e8b-b4dd-16f7323769d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13f8cc2a-4183-5051-a10e-eea4f7de407e" :question/math-academy-id "q-21528" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{3}-90x+108.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3})<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{3}-90x+108,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{4}+15{x}^{2}-90.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=20{x}^{3}+30x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =20{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{3}+30(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 3(-\\sqrt{3})+30(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =-90\\sqrt{3}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0d7f6c4-492c-5647-8c1b-3591f0f27fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303581} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ce5e96-8993-5aa8-bc53-0d0630c04716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303582} {:answer/id #uuid "e161399f-c327-5510-a9a3-de111d14051b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303583} {:answer/id #uuid "ad08725b-3681-51b8-aecb-52af410596c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303584} {:answer/id #uuid "a8147da4-a1f4-519d-962b-dc643ef43c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0d7f6c4-492c-5647-8c1b-3591f0f27fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303581} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb40351c-19e2-541d-80e2-bf69fe3ad102" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5be071e3-c224-574a-92ed-72af7b4c80c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-21541" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-2{x}^{5/3}+{x}^{2/3}.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{5})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{5})<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=\\frac{1}{5}$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=\\frac{1}{5}$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\frac{1}{5}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=-2{x}^{5/3}+{x}^{2/3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{2/3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-1/3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-\\frac{20}{9}{x}^{-1/3}-\\frac{2}{9}{x}^{-4/3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{5}) & =-\\frac{20}{9}\\cdot {5}^{1/3}-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {5}^{4/3}=-\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot {5}^{1/3}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e99781bc-0051-5212-a587-94137cb02638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303588} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ae39d9-85f8-5646-82c7-283ed1537163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303589} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5c4c6d-ebf3-5c84-931e-1c51c659550a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303590} {:answer/id #uuid "89ff8dbb-2320-585d-bd01-9dbda9b37b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303591} {:answer/id #uuid "f73e413d-44b1-55ff-9d1f-e977631eb493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f73e413d-44b1-55ff-9d1f-e977631eb493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303592} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3e15259-6670-524c-b39f-555d9daf423f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "93ba87ee-88a3-5ffc-9106-ee2a9e22d43b" :question/math-academy-id "q-21812" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59feeed0-e9be-5a46-b80b-e2e9fa2d6375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303595} {:answer/id #uuid "36f1c57b-21fc-512d-b4e8-c55c63b98cc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303596} {:answer/id #uuid "89e67402-095f-59dc-a7a3-f000d785249b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303597} {:answer/id #uuid "971ccc0d-1941-5879-a140-4c19d9e0a657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303598} {:answer/id #uuid "56d33001-3a5b-530a-929f-6b2ad5e2eaf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "971ccc0d-1941-5879-a140-4c19d9e0a657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303598} :answer-field/id #uuid "73c5b1af-5758-545b-8b28-55dcc88ee8d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "92b9bf0a-4f53-5ec3-9401-f90bf530cbdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-22164" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+4x+16.$  Given that ${f}^{′}(-1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Calculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+4x+16$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+12x+4=4(x+1)^{3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12(x+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ in the second derivative,   we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =12(1-1)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find the stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x+1)^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=-1.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=-2,0$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =4(-2+1)^{3}=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =4(0+1)^{3}=4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative changes the sign at  $x=-1$ from minus to plus, we conclude that $x=-1$  is a local minimum\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c801277-a1c8-5edd-bf38-9ac705fdc6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303602} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d45fe2-aea5-508a-b5de-7fcc578dbedc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303603} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e6e92c-686e-5c42-ad70-72f9c74740a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303604} {:answer/id #uuid "b4af0341-d7f9-5cab-bdb7-0fbffef2ea38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303605} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8a7058-9b41-5eb8-aea3-da5086245ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1e6e92c-686e-5c42-ad70-72f9c74740a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303604} :answer-field/id #uuid "3376b74f-267c-5c52-9ee9-bb0b7ce9b385" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8833706a-3d5b-5144-a18f-29a4115fa388" :question/math-academy-id "q-22197" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{7}-\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{7}-\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=7{x}^{6}-14{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=42{x}^{5}-70{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =42(0)^{5}-70(0)^{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{6}-14{x}^{5} & =0 \\\\ 7{x}^{5}(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =7(-1)^{5}(-1-2)=21>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =7(1)^{5}(1-2)=-7<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =7(3)^{5}(3-2)=7\\cdot {3}^{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7e2031-2b58-53cf-9313-757973d705c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303609} {:answer/id #uuid "b48d71f2-bcf9-527c-967d-f2da1e24f419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303610} {:answer/id #uuid "5d015514-a7b3-5266-9a45-b8ab27689308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303611} {:answer/id #uuid "73bce9d8-b1ee-5809-8a31-f8bcb11f2618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303612} {:answer/id #uuid "2edabbe6-4067-566d-9f8d-56bf73aa8a17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2edabbe6-4067-566d-9f8d-56bf73aa8a17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303613} :answer-field/id #uuid "14d60275-48d0-5909-b894-a30cdef9b718" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4ced9b91-c1a1-5092-a2cc-0b6989d8b2d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-2455" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=3x-{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)>0$\n- $x=-1$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=3x-{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3-3{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(1-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3(1-x)(1+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm 1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-6x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =6>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-6<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=-1,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."}]}
